  rewriting the notification layer once the real ones land.

  Before decomposing further, one question decides most of the architecture — and it's the one where guessing would cost
  you the most rework.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether two different people ever look at the same task. That single fact determines whether this needs a
  server at all, and almost nothing about a notification system survives switching between the answers later.

  - [1mMulti-user[0m — buys real "someone else changed your task" notifications. Costs a backend, auth, a database, and a
    push/poll transport. Rules out staying a static page; adds hosting and session security. Expensive to retrofit and
    expensive to remove.
  - [1mSingle-user, one browser[0m — buys a working app with zero infrastructure (localStorage, no accounts). Costs the
    ability to ever notify you about [3manother person's[0m edit; "notifications" shrinks to due-date reminders and
    self-generated events. Cheap now, but going multi-user later is close to a rewrite of the data layer.
  - [1mSingle-user, synced across devices[0m — middle ground: your tasks follow you, reminders can fire server-side. Costs a
    backend and auth but no sharing/permission model.

  [1mMy recommendation: start single-user, one browser[0m — unless sharing is actually the point. Your phrasing ("tasks [3mthey[0m
  care about") hints at multi-user, but I'd rather you confirm that than have me build auth on a hunch. If it is
  multi-user, we scope subsystems 1–3 honestly instead of pretending notifications is a small add-on.
